1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:08,130
If you've been carefully pronouncing City exactly as the dictionary tells you — "si-ti" — you've actually been completely fooled.

2
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:10,820
I actually heard this story from a friend not long ago.

3
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:16,690
She was traveling and asked an American where Citibank was, and he couldn't understand her at all.

4
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:21,860
So I asked her, did you pronounce it carefully, like "si... ti"?

5
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:26,040
She said, of course — it's spelled C-I-T-Y, so how else would you read it?

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:36,210
After hearing that, I pulled apart dozens of slow-motion videos to see exactly how native speakers move their mouths when they actually say this word.

7
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:43,010
The result shocked even me — nine times out of ten, they weren't saying "si-ti," they were saying something closer to "si-ri."

8
00:00:43,010 --> 00:00:48,640
The spelling clearly has a T — so why does it keep sounding like an "r" sound?

9
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,210
Once you learn this one principle today,

10
00:00:51,210 --> 00:00:59,020
water, better, even brand-new words — they'll come out of your mouth naturally, no dictionary required.

11
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:05,450
Even for native speakers, the tongue is originally supposed to press firmly against the roof of the mouth and release with a sharp "tuh" sound for T.

12
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:13,660
But when that T gets sandwiched between two vowels, and the vowel after it is unstressed, the tongue just doesn't bother stopping there.

13
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:16,370
It just flicks across the roof of the mouth and keeps going.

14
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:21,460
That flicking sound is what registers to our ears as an "r" or a soft "d."

15
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:30,190
So water isn't "wa-ter," it's "wa-rer"; city isn't "si-ti," it's "si-ri"; better isn't "be-ter," it's "be-rer."

16
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:37,650
Before I knew this principle, I used to memorize these three words separately — turns out they all come down to the exact same reason.

17
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:43,090
So does this shortcut also work for other words, like "letter," the one you use when writing a letter?

18
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:57,160
matter isn't "ma-ter," it's "ma-rer"; pretty isn't "pri-ti," it's "pri-di"; thirty isn't "ther-ti," it's "ther-ri"; letter isn't "le-ter," it's "le-rer."

19
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:04,660
It's all the same reason — whenever an unstressed vowel follows a T that's caught between two vowels, the tongue just flicks right past it.

20
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:07,970
But here's something that's genuinely surprising.

21
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:14,750
The T in "write" and the D in "ride" both turn into this exact same tongue-flick sound between vowels.

22
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:23,650
So "writer," someone who writes, and "rider," someone who rides, end up sounding almost indistinguishable in real native pronunciation.

23
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:28,250
Now, just watching this and thinking "huh, interesting" won't make it come out of your own mouth.

24
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:34,570
You have to actually say it out loud for it to become truly yours. Come on, say it with me — just three times.

25
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:44,600
wa-rer, wa-rer, wa-rer. si-ri, si-ri, si-ri. be-rer, be-rer, be-rer.

26
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:48,730
Relax your tongue completely, and let it flick lightly.

27
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:54,190
How was it — getting a feel for it? It's totally fine if it doesn't feel natural yet.

28
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:57,990
Let's practice once more with the other words we just learned.

29
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:11,750
ma-rer, ma-rer, ma-rer. pri-di, pri-di, pri-di. ther-ri, ther-ri, ther-ri. le-rer, le-rer, le-rer.

30
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,420
At first, everyone's tongue is going to feel stiff.

31
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:19,910
But repeat this for just a few days, and before you know it, your mouth will start moving that way on its own.

32
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:26,510
Alright, one more — remember I said "write" and "ride" sound almost identical?

33
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:34,700
Say it out loud yourself and you'll actually feel why. wri-rer, wri-rer. wri-rer, wri-rer.

34
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:41,160
You can barely tell them apart, right? Now let's pick up the pace, closer to real conversation speed.

35
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:50,920
wa-rer, si-ri, be-rer, ma-rer. One more time, faster. wa-rer, si-ri, be-rer, ma-rer.

36
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:56,670
It's okay if it feels awkward at first — feel free to replay just this part as many times as you need until it sticks.

37
00:03:56,670 --> 00:04:02,940
Here's the thing — in real conversation, these sounds never come one word at a time.

38
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:10,560
They're mixed into whole sentences and fly by in an instant. So let's test your real-world sense with one full sentence.

39
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:13,630
"The water in this city gets better every year."

40
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:23,060
This one sentence hides today's shortcut three times — water is "wa-rer," city is "si-ri," better is "be-rer."

41
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:27,860
Let's hear it once more, slowly, and this time focus only on those three sounds.

42
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:32,160
Hearing it inside a sentence feels different, doesn't it? Let's try one more sentence.

43
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:37,500
"This letter looks pretty good." And, "It doesn't matter, we still have thirty minutes."

44
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:43,830
These two sentences also naturally blend in "le-rer," "pri-di," "ma-rer," and "ther-ri."

45
00:04:43,830 --> 00:04:51,010
If you keep listening and repeating at the sentence level like this, at some point your ears will start catching the sound before you even think about it.

46
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:57,440
Now let's flip it around. I'll read that first sentence again, but with exactly one word missing.

47
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:01,580
See if you can guess what word belongs there, just from the rhythm of the sound.

48
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:08,140
"The... in this city gets better every year." What was the answer? That's right, water.

49
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:14,670
Even without knowing the spelling, if you catch the sentence's rhythm and that little flicking feel, you can absolutely get it right.

50
00:05:14,670 --> 00:05:21,680
One more. "This letter looks... good." What was the word this time? That's right, pretty.

51
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,650
If you got both of them right, that sound has genuinely started catching in your ear now.

52
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:36,500
Even if each individual word sounds unfamiliar at first, keep listening through sentences and rhythm like this, and before long the whole sentence will start sounding natural to your ear.

53
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:40,480
But does this shortcut really work for every single word?

54
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:43,200
No — there's exactly one case where it doesn't.

55
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:54,290
"atom," meaning the particle, becomes "a-rum" instead of "a-tum" — so why does "atomic," meaning "of the atom," stay as "a-ta-mic"?

56
00:05:54,290 --> 00:06:00,900
There's one reason — when the syllable right after the T carries the stress, the tongue never just flicks past it.

57
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:08,040
In "atom," the syllable after the T is unstressed, so it flicks — but in "atomic," the stress lands right on the "tom" syllable after the T, so the tongue has to actually stop there.

58
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:20,100
A stressed syllable has to be spoken clearly and with force, so no matter how lazy the tongue is, it can't take the shortcut there.

59
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:26,240
So there's really just one rule — it's not whether the T is between two vowels, it's whether there's stress right after it.

60
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:30,270
But did you know this T can sometimes disappear entirely, sound and all?

61
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:38,640
That's right — when an N comes right before the T, like in "winter" or "center," the T sound itself can vanish completely.

62
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:42,730
That's why "winter" can sometimes sound just like "winner."

63
00:06:42,730 --> 00:06:48,350
But this is a separate phenomenon from the vowel-to-vowel T we learned about today.

64
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:52,300
It's an optional habit — some people use it, some don't —

65
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:56,140
so you don't need to force it into the principle we learned today.

66
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:01,340
Now let's check with just one question whether you've really understood what we covered today.

67
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:13,370
Can you spot the exception we just learned on your own? I'll show you four words you've all probably used before: meeting, daughter, computer, hotel.

68
00:07:13,370 --> 00:07:21,760
Exactly one of these doesn't follow today's shortcut. Which one is it? I'll give you three seconds.

69
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,450
The answer is hotel.

70
00:07:24,450 --> 00:07:31,520
The stress lands right on the "tel" syllable after the T, so it skips the shortcut and stays as "ho-tel."

71
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:37,590
The other three all have an unstressed syllable right after, so they become "mee-ring," "daw-rer," and "com-pyu-rer."

72
00:07:37,590 --> 00:07:40,920
Here's everything we learned today, summed up in one card.

73
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:46,130
A T caught between two vowels has the tongue flick right past it, sounding close to an "r."

74
00:07:46,130 --> 00:07:50,540
But if the stress lands on the syllable right after the T, it stays exactly as it is.

75
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:52,460
Just these two lines are enough.

76
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:56,100
Oh, by the way — I told that friend the whole story later.

77
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:03,010
And now she says "Citibank" and "water" completely naturally, flowing right off her own tongue.

78
00:08:03,010 --> 00:08:06,110
Thank you for all your hard work today, after a long day.

79
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:14,820
Now, for water, city, even brand-new words — just relax your tongue and let it flick through comfortably.

80
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:21,920
And if R and L still trip you up, go check out that earlier video about the lazy tongue too.

81
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:26,180
Today, your mouth got one step more comfortable. See you next time.
